ntoxicated with love's cup the two  worlds have passed out of my kin  I am so drunken in this world  that except of drunkenness and rivarly I  have no tale to tell  in this verse he expresses both  intoxication and love for God but also a  hint or indication of a unity and  connection between creator and created  in fact one can say semi problematically  that Sufism has tended to emphasize the  imminence of God in the world more than  other denominations this became perhaps  most prominently expressed by one of  Rumi's contemporaries Mohideen ibn  al-arabi and his followers in the school  that followed him in what became later  known as wada thelusian or the unity of  being empanada B presents a cosmology  were in terms of being or who should God  is actually everything that exists and  the world are his manifestations he  writes the following the world is  illusory it has no real existence you  have been made to imagine that the world  is something separate and independently  real outside of the absolute but in  reality this is not so now a banana B  should not be understood as a pantheist  or even panin theist as he very much  still maintains the transcendence and  incomparability of God to the world  while at the same time affirming his  eminence and sameness needless to say  this doctrine has been very  controversial and well it 'banahna be  himself maybe did not hear to the  philosophy of what outside were Jude as  it became known later many of his  followers did and it became a  significant part of later Sufi  philosophy discussions about what it  means and how it should be understood  has been a source of great debate  throughout history to his followers like  the famous Persian poet Jame he's known  as sheikh al akbar or the great master  the greatest master while for people  like eben tamiya which was his opponent  and the modern Wahhabi movements he is  often considered a heretic indeed since  medieval times and the influence of some  of the people I've talked about Sufism  eventually grew to become the form of  Islam practiced by the majority of the  world's Muslims right up until about the  middle or late 19th century and in some  parts of the world it still holds this  position for example in Senegal in which  92 percent of the population is Muslim  95% of those Muslims belonged to a Sufi  Brotherhood  order in other words Sufism is certainly  not a small sect of Islam as some people  tend to believe generally speaking there  are a lot of misunderstandings about  Sufism both in the non-muslim and Muslim  world as a result of some let's say  liberal translations of brumis work for  example many people have come to believe  that Sufism is something separated from  Islam and some form of general  spirituality but when we consider its  history we can clearly see that its  teachings are firmly rooted in Islam and  that the poetry of Rumi for example is  is often based on the Quran or the  hadith with that said within the limits  of Islam Sufism has devolved in many  different directions and taken on many  many forms throughout history it isn't  just a tradition consisting of ascetic  individuals living in poverty it's been  practiced by kings landowners military  personnel and people from all walks of  life any generalizations like this  including that Sufism is a peaceful  version of Islam should be viewed very  cautiously nonetheless Sufism is still  strong today and many of the old tariq  al Zour orders still have a large  following and some new ones have even  popped up from time to time like the  titania or the meridian in West Africa  Sufism is also often associated with  music today as many Sufi orders have  used music in their rituals for example  there is usand or from Senegal who  belonged to the Meridian and has had a  successful career in the West by among  other things working with people like  Peter Gabriel in his songs he often  sings about very important people from  West African Sufism like the Mara boots  or sheiks and also the prophets of Islam  he even has an album called Egypt that  is specifically written about Islam and  Sufism another good example is the  qawalli singer Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan  from the shish dia order he became  famous in the late 80s and 90s through  albums like most most there is more  interest in Sufism in the West generally  partly stemming from the popularity of  Rumi's poetry and also to some because a  new age interpretations of Sufism its  enormous diversity is really difficult  to grasp and a short video like this is  really insufficient to talk about it all  but with its increased presence in  Europe and North America  unfortunately also some news coverage  about violence committed against Sufis  like the mosque attack in Egypt back in  November  it seems relevant that we try to  understand its complex history and  different expressions after all things  are rarely very rarely as simple as we  like to believe I'll see you next time  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

